# Map And Reduce

`map` and `reduce` process an ordered collection inside a Flow. Map calls one
target for each item. Reduce sends the current accumulator and one item to its
target. Each form is one public Flow node.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define Item Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.DoubleItem do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_collections_double_item",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        value: Zoi.integer(),
        index: Zoi.integer(),
        item_id: Zoi.string()
      }),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        value: Zoi.integer(),
        index: Zoi.integer(),
        item_id: Zoi.string()
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value} = params, _context) do
    {:ok, %{params | value: value * 2}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowCollections.AddToTotal do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_collections_add_to_total",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer(), value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{total: total, value: value}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total: total + value}}
  end
end
```

## Map A Collection

The short form contains these fields:

```text
map "node_name",
  collection: input(:items),
  action: MyApp.Actions.ProcessItem,
  params: %{item: item()},
  on_error: :fail_fast,
  after: "optional_predecessor"
```

`on_error` and `after` are optional. The default error mode is `:fail_fast`.
The collection must resolve to a proper list.

Map `params` can use:

- `item()` for the complete item;
- `item(path)` for a path inside the item;
- `item_index()` for the zero-based source index; and
- `item_id()` for the stable identity of the Map item.

## Reduce A Collection

Reduce needs an initial accumulator. Its `params` can also use
`accumulator()` or `accumulator(path)`.

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.DoubleValues do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_collections_double_values",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{values: Zoi.list(Zoi.integer())}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    map "double_values",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowCollections.DoubleItem,
      params: %{
        value: item(),
        index: item_index(),
        item_id: item_id()
      }

    reduce "sum_values" do
      collection(result("double_values"))
      initial(%{total: 0})
      action(FlowCollections.AddToTotal)
      params(%{total: accumulator(:total), value: item(:value)})
    end

    output %{
      mapped: result("double_values"),
      total: result("sum_values")
    }
  end
end
```

Map returns an aggregate with `kind`, `results`, and `errors`. Each successful
record contains `index`, `item_id`, and `output`.

```elixir
{:ok,
 %{
   mapped: %{kind: :jido_flow_map_result, results: results, errors: []},
   total: %{total: 12}
 }} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowCollections.DoubleValues, %{values: [1, 2, 3]}, %{})

[0, 1, 2] = Enum.map(results, & &1.index)
[2, 4, 6] = Enum.map(results, & &1.output.value)
true = Enum.all?(results, &is_binary(&1.item_id))
```

Reduce can consume a complete successful Map aggregate. Each ordered Map
output becomes one Reduce item. Reduce rejects a direct Map aggregate that
contains collected errors.

Reduce processes items in source order and always runs serially. An empty Map
returns an empty aggregate. An empty Reduce returns the valid initial value.

## Collect Map Errors

`:collect_errors` completes all item work. Successful records stay in
`results`. Failed records stay in `errors`.

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.RejectNegative do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_collections_reject_negative",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context) when value >= 0, do: {:ok, %{value: value * 2}}
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:error, "negative value"}
end

defmodule FlowCollections.CollectFailures do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_collections_collect_failures",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{values: Zoi.list(Zoi.integer())}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    map "check_values",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowCollections.RejectNegative,
      params: %{value: item()},
      on_error: :collect_errors
  end
end

{:ok, %{results: [%{index: 0, output: %{value: 4}}], errors: [failure]}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowCollections.CollectFailures, %{values: [2, -1]}, %{})

1 = failure.index
%Jido.Action.Error.ExecutionFailureError{} = failure.error
```

`:fail_fast` stops Map at the first failed source item in serial execution. In
asynchronous execution, it completes the current bounded work window and
reports the lowest failed source index.

## Run Map Items Concurrently

Map uses Flow execution options. `async: false` runs item calls serially. With
`async: true`, independent item calls can run concurrently.

```elixir
{:ok, %{total: %{total: 20}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowCollections.DoubleValues,
    %{values: [1, 2, 3, 4]},
    %{},
    async: true,
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

Map records stay in source-index order. Task completion order does not change
the result order. Reduce stays serial.

## Local Reference Scope

| Reference | Map `params` | Reduce `params` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `item`, `item_index`, `item_id` | Yes | Yes |
| `accumulator` | No | Yes |

Map and Reduce `collection` fields cannot use local references. Reduce
`initial` also cannot use them. These fields can use Flow input, context,
literals, and prior node results.

## Build Collections At Runtime

`Jido.Flow.Builder` has the same canonical expression model, but uses normal
function calls because it builds data at runtime:

```elixir
alias Jido.Flow.Builder

builder =
  Builder.new(name: "runtime_collections")
  |> Builder.map(
    :double_values,
    Builder.input(:values),
    FlowCollections.DoubleItem,
    %{
      value: Builder.item(),
      index: Builder.item_index(),
      item_id: Builder.item_id()
    }
  )
  |> Builder.reduce(
    :sum_values,
    Builder.result(:double_values),
    Builder.value(%{total: 0}),
    FlowCollections.AddToTotal,
    %{total: Builder.accumulator(:total), value: Builder.item(:value)}
  )
  |> Builder.return(Builder.result(:sum_values))

{:ok, runtime_flow} = Builder.build(builder)
{:ok, %{total: 12}} = Jido.Exec.run(runtime_flow, %{values: [1, 2, 3]}, %{})
```

Map and Reduce each appear as one node in `Jido.Exec.ready/1`, `step/2`, and
`wave/1`. The public execution API does not pause between items.

Continue with [Iterate And State](flow-loops-state.livemd) for bounded State
transitions.
